and the physical field contributes $-2m(P_{1} + P_{2})$, zero at $m = 0$. Summing, with $\sum_{i \geq 1}c_{i} = -1$, gives the stated limit; the regulator masses and the individual weights drop out (checked: three weight sets with $\sum c_{i} = 0$ give $	frac12$; the rational part $	frac{1}{32}$ of the integral is recomputed from the radial integral $\int_{0}^{\infty}r^{3}(r^{2} + \Delta)^{-3}dr = 1/(4\Delta)$, whose antiderivative is checked exactly, the angular volume $2\pi^{2}$ and $(2\pi)^{-4}$). The overall Feynman-rule factors of the amplitude ($-1$ for the loop, the units of propagators and vertices) multiply $T$ and are not part of the statement. The magnitude $1/(2\pi^{2})$ is the standard one of Adler and of Bell and Jackiw (Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-adler-bell-jackiw-axial-anomaly}, stated there in the index reading of the sources as $(\alpha/4\pi)\epsilon FF$; Peskin and Schroeder, ch. 19, cited from memory). That it does not depend on the regulator is not proved here: the regulator-free extension result (\claimref{synthesis.one-loop-vacuum-polarisation-extends-without-a-regulator-and-ward-preserving-extensions-differ-only-by-the-renormalisation-constants}, clause (iii)) shows that every covariant extension conserving both vector currents has the same axial divergence.

\emph{(iv) Field form.} $\langle I_{4}a_{1}k_{1}a_{2}k_{2}angle_{0}$ sees only $a_{1}\wedge k_{1}\wedge a_{2}\wedge k_{2}$, which is $\langle (a_{1}\wedge k_{1})(a_{2}\wedge k_{2})angle_{4}$; so it equals $\langle I_{4}f_{1}f_{2}angle_{0}$, and swapping the photons leaves it unchanged since $f_{1}f_{2}$ and $f_{2}f_{1}$ have the same grade-four part (checked). For $F = E + I_{4}B$ with $E = E^{k}\gamma_{k}\gamma_{0}$, $B = B^{k}\gamma_{k}\gamma_{0}$, $F^{2} = E^{2} - B^{2} + 2(E\cdot B)I_{4}$, so $\langle I_{4}F^{2}angle_{0} = -2E\cdot B$ (checked at two field values). Reading the momentum-space divergence as a field equation gives the standard $|\partial_{\mu}j_{5}^{\mu}| = 	frac{e^{2}}{2\pi^{2}}|E\cdot B|$ per Dirac fermion (argued, the usual Fourier translation; sign by convention).

\emph{(v) Chiral fields.} $P_{L}a = aP_{R}$ for a vector $a$ and $P_{R}\gamma^{
u}P_{L} = \gamma^{
u}P_{L}P_{L}$, so moving each projector to the right through the next vector flips it and the three collapse to one, $\mathrm{Tr}[\gamma^{\mu}P_{L}a\gamma^{
u}P_{L}b\gamma^{ho}P_{L}c] = \mathrm{Tr}[\gamma^{\mu}a\gamma^{
u}b\gamma^{ho}cP_{R}]$, which is half the vector trace plus half the $\gamma_{5}$ trace (checked on four index triples at a rational loop momentum). With group matrices at the vertices, the two orientations of the loop carry $\mathrm{tr}(T_{a}T_{b}T_{c})$ and $\mathrm{tr}(T_{a}T_{c}T_{b})$, and Bose symmetry of the external legs leaves the axial part proportional to $d_{abc} = \mathrm{tr}(T_{a}\{T_{b}, T_{c}\})$ (argued, not checked: the standard reduction, cited from memory). Clause (v) is therefore proved for the projector identity and argued for the reduction to $d$. Pauli-Villars regulators of a Weyl field are Dirac fields (Fact, clause (iii)), and the anomaly is their surviving mass term, as in (iii).

\emph{(vi) One generation.} The content is the particle half of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.anomaly-freedom-forces-slot-content}(ii) with $m = 1$, all left-handed. Represent $X = (A, B, y)$ with $A \in \mathfrak{sl}(3)$, $B \in \mathfrak{sl}(2)$, $y \in \C$ on each summand $(r_{3}, r_{2})_{Y}$ as $A \otimes 1 + 1 \otimes B + yY$, with $-A^{\mathrm{T}}$ on $ ar 3$: sixteen dimensions. The tensor $d$ is symmetric, so it vanishes exactly when its components on a basis vanish; on the basis of eight elementary and diagonal matrices of $\mathfrak{sl}(3)$, three of $\mathfrak{sl}(2)$ and one hypercharge, all 364 symmetric components are zero, and every basis element has trace zero (checked). For the hypercharge the last statement is $\mathrm{tr}\,Y = 0$, the mixed gravitational condition of the imported anomaly coefficients; it is a trace count on the content, not a computed gauge-gravitational triangle. This contains the four conditions of the stored theorem and the $[SU(3)]^{3}$ coefficient; the remaining components vanish because $\mathfrak{sl}(2)$ has no cubic invariant and the components with a single traceless factor vanish. A single field does not cancel ($\mathrm{tr}\,Y^{3} = 6/216$ on the quark doublet, checked), so the cancellation is between the fields of one generation and is independent of the number of generations. $\square$
